r drive’s controller to make the data readable again.</span></p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p data-path-to-node=\"16,3,0\"><b class=\"\" data-path-to-node=\"16,3,0\" data-index-in-node=\"0\">Final Verification and Data Delivery:</b><span class=\"\"> We do one last check to make sure your important files,</span><span class=\"\"> databases,</span><span class=\"\"> and media are actually working perfectly.</span><span class=\"\"> Then,</span><span class=\"\"> we move your data to a secure external drive for you to check.</span></p>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<h3 class=\"\" data-path-to-node=\"17\">Real-World Success: A Case Example</h3>\n<p data-path-to-node=\"18\">An architectural firm here in Mirdif called us in a panic. Their main workstation’s 2TB NVMe SSD just stopped working, and they had weeks of design files stuck on it right before a big deadline. The drive wouldn&#8217;t initialize because of a firmware error-which happens a lot with these fast drives. Our engineers performed a direct controller bypass and manually pulled the raw data from the chips in our clean-room. We managed to recover 100% of their project data in under 48 hours, saving them from a massive business disaster!</p>\n<h2 data-path-to-node=\"19\">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>\n<p data-path-to-node=\"20\"><b data-path-to-node=\"20\" data-index-in-node=\"0\">What should I do if my NVMe drive is suddenly not recognized?</b> Power down your computer right away and don&#8217;t try to boot from it again. Keeping it turned on can make firmware problems or electrical faults much worse.</p>\n<p data-path-to-node=\"21\"><b data-path-to-node=\"21\" data-index-in-node=\"0\">Is it safe to use standard recovery software on a dead NVMe drive?</b> We really don&#8217;t recommend it. If the BIOS can&#8217;t see the drive, software won&#8217;t be able to either. Plus, running extra programs can stress out a drive that&#8217;s already failing.</p>\n<p data-path-to-node=\"22\"><b data-path-to-node=\"22\" data-index-in-node=\"0\">Do y