e data is actually more fragile than regular office files because of how it’s recorded. Most systems use &#8220;loop recording,&#8221; which means they keep writing new video over the old stuff. If the system fails or someone</p>\n<p data-path-to-node=\"3\">deletes a file, every minute you leave the machine on makes it more likely the data will be overwritten forever. That&#8217;s why <b data-path-to-node=\"3\" data-index-in-node=\"467\">Security Camera Footage Recovery JLT</b> is really a time-sensitive emergency.</p>\n<h3 data-path-to-node=\"4\">Common Issues Leading to CCTV Data Loss</h3>\n<ul data-path-to-node=\"5\">\n<li>\n<p data-path-to-node=\"5,0,0\"><b data-path-to-node=\"5,0,0\" data-index-in-node=\"0\">Accidental Deletion:</b> It happens someone deletes a clip or a whole day while trying to save it to a USB.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p data-path-to-node=\"5,1,0\"><b data-path-to-node=\"5,1,0\" data-index-in-node=\"0\">Disk Overwriting:</b> The system just keeps recording and &#8220;loops&#8221; over the incident before you can back it up.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p data-path-to-node=\"5,2,0\"><b data-path-to-node=\"5,2,0\" data-index-in-node=\"0\">Physical Hard Drive Crash:</b> CCTV hard drives (CCTV HDD) work so hard; a mechanical failure in the DVR/NVR is a very common reason for loss.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p data-path-to-node=\"5,3,0\"><b data-path-to-node=\"5,3,0\" data-index-in-node=\"0\">File System Corruption:</b> A lot of systems use weird formats that a normal Windows computer can&#8217;t even read.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p data-path-to-node=\"5,4,0\"><b data-path-to-node=\"5,4,0\" data-index-in-node=\"0\">Firmware Failure:</b> Sometimes the software inside the box just glitches out and makes all the recordings invisible.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p data-path-to-node=\"5,5,0\"><b data-path-to-node=\"5,5,0\" data-index-in-node=\"0\">Intentional Sabotage:</b> In some cases, someone might try to format the disk to hide what they did.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p data-path-to-node=\"5