cal damage.</p>\n<h3 data-path-to-node=\"9\">Common Server and Storage Issues</h3>\n<p data-path-to-node=\"10,0,0\"><b data-path-to-node=\"10,0,0\" data-index-in-node=\"0\">RAID Array Collapse:</b> This involves more than one failed drive. Parity corruption or multiple disk drop-outs in RAID 5, 6, or 10 render the volume unmountable.</p>\n<p data-path-to-node=\"10,1,0\"><b data-path-to-node=\"10,1,0\" data-index-in-node=\"0\">Hypervisor &amp; VM Corruption:</b> Crashes within VMware ESXi or Microsoft Hyper-V trap virtual machines inside a corrupted host.</p>\n<p data-path-to-node=\"10,2,0\"><b data-path-to-node=\"10,2,0\" data-index-in-node=\"0\">Database Inaccessibility:</b> Logical corruption in SQL or Oracle databases prevents access to vital financial records.</p>\n<p data-path-to-node=\"10,3,0\"><b data-path-to-node=\"10,3,0\" data-index-in-node=\"0\">Controller Card Malfunction:</b> When the RAID controller firmware fails, the operating system loses the &#8220;map&#8221; to your data blocks.</p>\n<p data-path-to-node=\"10,4,0\"><b data-path-to-node=\"10,4,0\" data-index-in-node=\"0\">Thermal Damage &amp; Power Surges:</b> AC failure in a Dubai server room leads to rapid overheating. This causes catastrophic hardware warping or thermal head crashes.</p>\n<p data-path-to-node=\"10,5,0\"><b data-path-to-node=\"10,5,0\" data-index-in-node=\"0\">Human Error:</b> Accidental initialization or formatted partitions during routine maintenance can wipe entire directories.</p>\n<p data-path-to-node=\"10,6,0\"><b data-path-to-node=\"10,6,0\" data-index-in-node=\"0\">Ransomware &amp; Cyber Attacks:</b> Malicious actors target high-value corporate entities with server-wide encryption.</p>\n<p data-path-to-node=\"10,7,0\"><b data-path-to-node=\"10,7,0\" data-index-in-node=\"0\">Physical Hardware Wear:</b> Mechanical failure often hits high-speed SAS or enterprise-grade SSD drives after years of 24/7 operation.</p>\n<h2 data-path-to-node=\"12\">Why Top Palm Jumeirah Enterprise